+From 4d2c342d986302725b12a1b82448c0efe031fc91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
+Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:04:51 -0400
+Subject: [PATCH 52/52] ipa: simple: Add the Look algorithm and tune it on the
+ Pixel 7 Pro
+
+Drive the debayer's hue, vibrance and tone curve from a new Look
+algorithm: the tuning file gives the 12 hue sectors' shifts in degrees
+and vibrances, the chroma at which vibrance falls to none, and the
+shadow lift, which the AGC's output prediction follows.
+
+Tune it for the Google Pixel 7 Pro's four cameras on our own daylight
+shots, compared with our own shots of the same scenes from another
+phone. Measured there, greens came out 15-45 degrees (HSV) on the cyan
+side of the reference and with half its saturation, and blue skies on
+the violet side; reds were close. So: greens 15 degrees towards
+yellow, blue skies 5 out of violet, vibrance 1.0 for greens, 0.6-0.8
+for blues and none for reds and warm colours, and a lift of 0.45. An
+offline preview with twice the shifts and vibrance on the warm sectors
+turned skies cyan and gravel pink.
+
+Replayed offline on nine pairs of shots, the mean output moved from 32
+sRGB levels under the reference to within one (median within 6, 95th
+percentile 12 over) and the greens' saturation to the reference's; the
+greens' HSV hue moved from 15 to 11 degrees off and the sky's stayed
+about 15 off, which looks right rather than cyan.
+
+Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
